one \code{layer} picks by number, or by name among that dimension's values.
An object with several attributes and such a dimension draws the first
slice of the chosen attribute; with more than one dimension beyond \code{x}
and \code{y} of more than one element, slice it first (\code{x[, , , 1, 2]}, or
\code{dplyr::slice()}; a dimension of one element is not counted). A factor
attribute draws its codes, a \code{units} attribute its numbers.

\strong{Orientation.} 'stars' stores an attribute as an array with \code{x} first
and \code{y} second, and the grid's \code{y} \code{delta} is usually negative (the
offset is the top edge). The matrix route takes rows from the top, so
the array is transposed here, and flipped when \code{delta} is positive (or
negative for \code{x}): \code{x[[1]][i, j]} is the cell at column \code{i} from the
left and row \code{j} from the \code{y} offset, as 'stars' means it, whichever
way the offsets run.

\strong{Out of scope for now.} Curvilinear grids (\code{x} and \code{y} as matrices),
rectilinear grids (an \code{x} or \code{y} dimension with explicit cell edges
rather than one \code{delta}), sheared or rotated grids (a non-zero affine),
and vector data cubes (a dimension of simple features) are not drawn
(allboa/design decision 0010, item 5); each is an error that says so.
