\code{\link[stars:st_warp]{stars::st_warp()}} first, or draw a vector cube's geometries with
\code{view(sf::st_as_sf(x))}.

\strong{CRS.} The view CRS follows \code{\link[=view_crs]{view_crs()}}'s rule from the object's own
CRS (\code{\link[sf:st_crs]{sf::st_crs()}}), as for a \code{SpatRaster}: a projected CRS is kept, a
lon/lat grid south of 40S or north of 60N is drawn in a polar view. An
object with no CRS is an error. A \code{stars} object has an extent and CRS
of its own, so it can be an element of \code{view(list(...))} and added to a
view with \code{\link[=view_add]{view_add()}} with no further argument.
